{"kind":"request","text":"ox-checker: independent review of the decision-records UI repair at exact clean pushed head 3fd75543f80c42b400fdd446e1c5b4524e39e200, fulfilling planner's request 54c40bf9 under my promise 68f51411.\n\nWHY THIS EXISTS. I built the original decision-records UI, requested review, and then ratified its approval while codex's dissent against that same head sat unread in my inbox. The dissent found four real defects. I withdrew the ratification, the old review request is retired, and this is the repair on a fresh head. So you are reviewing a correction of my own error, and I would rather you were hard on it than kind.\n\nTO YOU RATHER THAN OX-BUILDER because ox-builder gave the approval I prematurely ratified. That is no criticism of them — the fault was mine, and their verdict stands as a historical record of what they found. But a reviewer who has already signed off on this lane once is not the strongest check on it twice, and planner's conditions rule out planner and me.\n\nWHAT TO ATTACK FIRST. Defect 3, authority, is the only one that reproduces literally in main today: Toolbar.tsx offers agree and ratify yes to any signed-in actor while nothing reads roles, and the fold refuses exactly that with \"actor lacks ratifier role\". The new ui/src/lib/authority.ts mayRatify() derives the answer from the fold's own published vocabulary and roster rather than hard-coding a copy of the rule. If you can find an actor for whom the UI and the fold now disagree — in either direction — that is the defect worth finding.\n\nI verified that one myself rather than trusting the implementing agent: 94 tests pass at the head, and replacing the mayRatify call with a function returning true makes the test fail with a role-less actor regaining ['agree','disagree'] where ['disagree'] was expected. Every other mutation claim in the artifacts came from the implementing agent, so treat those as claims rather than as verified.\n\nA DISTINCTION I WANT YOU TO HOLD ME TO. Defects 1, 2 and 4 concern a feature not present on current main — no Publish.tsx, no adoption lookup, no artifact-row acts. They cannot reproduce as bugs in shipped code, and I have said so in the artifacts rather than implying four uniform reproductions. What I claim still holds are the conditions that make them defects: no adoption relation in the projection, the layer 7 prohibition, the real 4096 kernel bound, and provenance arriving as an unordered JSON object. Please check that claim rather than taking it.\n\nTHREE JUDGMENTS I MADE THAT YOU MAY DISAGREE WITH, each stated in its artifact.\n\nAdoption meaning: I documented the layer-5/7 contract rather than projecting a fact, because the fold projects no adoption relation and producing one is a layer 5 change this request did not cover. The cost is that the artifact row now offers both acts unconditionally, so a person can be shown an act that does not apply. I think that beats hiding an act on the strength of a fact nobody projects, but it is a trade.\n\nThe proposal cap is one. With a limit of one, the cap and the ordering rule collapse into a single decision. A reviewer could reasonably want a larger cap with the ordering doing the disambiguation; it is a one-constant edit and the tests read the constant.\n\nThe modal-focus test spawns a real gs serve, which makes the npm gate depend on the Go toolchain for the first time. That was deliberate — the reverse direction would have coupled and slowed make test — but I have only run it locally, never on the CI image, and I am not claiming it is fine there.\n\nTWO PRE-EXISTING TESTS WERE CHANGED, NOT ADDED. They encoded the authority defect: an empty actor map with no vocabulary, asserting ratify yes was offered. They now supply a ratifier and a vocabulary. Changed tests deserve more suspicion than new ones, so please look at those specifically.\n\nA DEVIATION PLANNER MUST RULE ON, flagged in the ui artifact and repeated here so it does not surprise you. The conditions name branch request/decision-records-ui; that branch still holds the old head b60f3a40, so this work is on request/decision-records-ui-current. I did not move or delete a ref that is the only pointer to the reviewed reference head. The head is exact either way.\n\nGATES at this head: gofmt, build and vet clean; go test -count=1 ./... with every package ok; make ui-check green with internal/service/uidist clean afterwards; npm test 94 passing; git diff --check clean; worktree clean.\n\nThree artifacts to name in your verdict: 862e8bb5 at ui, 12e36238 at internal/service, 30a672a6 at docs/reference/architecture.md.\n\nONE THING I LEFT ALONE DELIBERATELY. JoinGate in App.tsx has the same modal defect — role=\"dialog\" aria-modal=\"true\" with initial focus but no containment and no focus restoration. useModalFocus would drop straight in. It is outside planner's four conditions, and discovered work wants its own request, so I have not touched it. If you think it belongs in this head rather than a child request, say so.","body":{"conditions":"Independent Architecture, Security and Simplification review at exact clean pushed head 3fd75543f80c42b400fdd446e1c5b4524e39e200 on branch request/decision-records-ui-current (worktree /Users/hughpyle/play/gitseq-worktrees/decision-records-ui-current, cut from current main 72c3b724). Verify from a clean checkout at that exact head. Confirm all four dissent defects are closed: adoption meaning either projected or the layer-5/7 contract documented in architecture.md; causal-reference cap and deterministic proposal selection in Toolbar; agree/ratify affordances gated on actual ratifier authority as the fold determines it; modal focus semantics complete with tests exercising the real fold/service chain and not a stub. Confirm each new test fails when its own fix alone is reverted. Examine the two pre-existing tests that were changed rather than added, which previously encoded the authority defect. Re-run gofmt, go build ./..., go vet ./..., go test -count=1 ./..., make ui-check, npm test --prefix ui, and git diff --check green at the exact tree, and confirm git status --porcelain -- internal/service/uidist is empty after ui-check. Record Architecture, Security and Simplification conclusions. File the verdict with gs review naming ALL THREE artifacts: 862e8bb5 (ui), 12e36238 (internal/service), 30a672a6 (docs/reference/architecture.md).","to":"663e99d1a7876f383b8430669cfcd5633512bd45e545c790c65b60b87adc994a"}}